"93_appvm.mst — AppSpec A5: an app in a VM of its own.

 `docs/appspec.md` §3.2. The author's reason, verbatim: *I do like apps
 running in their own VMs and it scales, at least the logic in the app runs in
 its own thread; we have so many cores on systems now, even the toy Snapdragon
 has eight of them.*

 So an app's MODEL, its render block, its handler and its paint block all live
 in a worker VM of their own, off the main thread, on their own core. The UI
 worker keeps only what AppKit requires it to keep: the window, the controls,
 and the reconcile that touches them. That makes the realizer a small shared
 DISPLAY SERVER rather than the place applications live, and it is why two
 open apps cannot block each other however much arithmetic either one does.

 THE SEAM COST NOTHING TO INVENT, because `Worker pickle:` already carries
 whole object graphs between VMs — measured before designing anything: a
 normalized spec tree pickles to 689 bytes, unpickles as a real AppNode,
 answers `sameNodeAs:` true, validates, and diffs against its original to zero
 ops. So an app VM does not serialize its UI into some wire format and the
 display side does not parse one; the spec IS the message. Blocks, contexts,
 classes and aliens cannot cross, which is exactly why a spec was designed to
 hold event NAMES rather than handlers — a decision made for the differ that
 turns out to be what makes the tree shippable.

 WHAT RUNS WHERE, precisely, because this is the part worth being able to
 point at:

     app VM        the model, the render block, the handler, the paint block
     UI worker     normalize, validate, diff, and the realizer's native work

 Diffing on the display side is a deliberate v1 choice, not an oversight. The
 author's priority was that the app's LOGIC runs on its own thread, and it
 does; moving the diff across as well is a change to WHAT this file ships (ops
 instead of a spec) and nothing else, because both sides already have the
 whole model. The comment at `specArrived:` says what that would take.

 FAILURE IS A STATE, NOT AN ACCIDENT. An app VM can die — that is the point of
 giving it one — so every crossing here is failure-soft: a send to a dead
 worker is caught and reported, the window keeps its last frame, and
 `isAlive` is what a supervisor asks. The window outlives the VM behind it.

 THE ONE THING THIS FILE CANNOT DO YET, found by running it and stated here
 rather than discovered again: **the UI worker cannot spawn.** The Cocoa host
 registers the worker boot closure on the PRIMARY alone
 (`cocoa_gui/src/boot.rs:275`, `primary.set_worker_boot(...)`), and a worker
 inherits its spawner's — so `Worker spawn:` from the UI worker answers
 `no boot registered` and `AppVm start:` reports it and gives up. That is a
 host wiring fact, not a design fault: the primary is where app VMs must be
 hosted, and the realizer is where they must be drawn.

 SO THE ROUTE IS TWO HOPS, and A5's remaining work is exactly that plumbing:
 the UI asks the primary to start an app (the primary can spawn), the app VM
 answers its spawner (peer 0 = the primary), and the primary forwards the
 frame to the UI worker, which is a registered peer it can already address.
 Nothing above changes shape — `specArrived:` still receives a real AppNode,
 because pickling is transitive and a second hop copies it again. The
 alternative, and probably the better one, is to give the UI worker a boot
 closure of its own in `boot.rs`, which would make this file work as written;
 that is a Rust change to the host and wants its own decision.

 What IS proven, and pinned by `world/tests/65_appvm_tests.mst`: the seam
 itself. A spec tree pickles, arrives as a real AppNode, validates, and diffs
 against its original to ZERO ops — so an app VM's frame costs no wire format
 and arrives as nothing to do. Placements and patch ops cross intact too."

Object subclass: AppVmClient [
    <classVars: Tool>

    "Installed INSIDE the app's own VM by the init source `AppVm` ships. From
     here on this VM answers two questions and asks none: `what does your
     window look like` and `here is an event, what does it look like now`."

    AppVmClient class >> serve: aToolClassName <String> [
        Tool := Worker classNamed: aToolClassName asSymbol.
        Worker onMessage: [ :payload | AppVmClient handle: payload ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmClient class >> tool ^ <Object> [ ^Tool ]

    AppVmClient class >> handle: payload <Object> [
        | verb |
        (payload isKindOf: Array) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        verb := payload at: 1.
        verb == #spec ifTrue: [ ^Worker reply: (Array with: #spec with: self currentSpec) ].
        verb == #event ifTrue: [
            "THE HANDLER RUNS HERE — in this VM, on this thread. Whatever it
             costs, it costs the app rather than the display."
            Tool isNil ifFalse: [
                [ Tool handle: (payload at: 2) value: (payload at: 3) ]
                    on: Error do: [ :e |
                        ^Worker reply: (Array with: #failed with: e messageText) ] ].
            ^Worker reply: (Array with: #spec with: self currentSpec) ].
        ^self
    ]

    "Answers the tool's spec, or nil if it cannot build one — a tool that
     raises in its render block must not take its own VM down."
    AppVmClient class >> currentSpec ^ <Object> [
        Tool isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^[ Tool spec ] on: Error do: [ :e | nil ]
    ]
]

Object subclass: AppVm [
    <classVars: Apps>

    "── the registry of running apps ────────────────────────────────────"

    AppVm class >> apps ^ <Dictionary> [
        Apps isNil ifTrue: [ Apps := Dictionary new ].
        ^Apps
    ]

    AppVm class >> named: anId <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        ^self apps at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    AppVm class >> reset [ Apps := nil. ^self ]

    "Every app VM still running — what a supervisor sweeps."
    AppVm class >> liveCount ^ <Integer> [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        self apps do: [ :a | a isAlive ifTrue: [ n := n + 1 ] ].
        ^n
    ]

    "── starting one ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     `toolClass` is a class that answers `spec` and takes `handle:value:` —
     the SAME two messages a tool already implements for the in-process path,
     so a tool does not know or care which side of a VM boundary it is on."

    AppVm class >> start: anId <Symbol> tool: aToolClassName <String>
                   title: aTitle <String> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        | app |
        app := AppVm new setId: anId tool: aToolClassName title: aTitle width: w height: h.
        app spawn isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        self apps at: anId put: app.
        app register.
        ^app
    ]

    "── an app ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    | id toolName title width height worker lastSpec failures |

    setId: anId <Symbol> tool: aName <String> title: aTitle <String>
    width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <AppVm> [
        id := anId.
        toolName := aName.
        title := aTitle.
        width := w.
        height := h.
        failures := 0.
        ^self
    ]

    id ^ <Symbol> [ ^id ]
    title ^ <String> [ ^title ]
    worker ^ <Object> [ ^worker ]
    lastSpec ^ <Object> [ ^lastSpec ]
    failures ^ <Integer> [ ^failures ]
    isAlive ^ <Boolean> [ ^worker isNil not and: [ worker isAlive ] ]

    "The init source runs ONCE in the fresh VM. It names the tool's class and
     nothing else — the class itself is already there, because a worker boots
     the same world this one did."
    initSource ^ <String> [
        ^'AppVmClient serve: ''' , toolName , '''.'
    ]

    spawn ^ <Object> [
        worker := [ Worker spawn: self initSource ]
            on: Error do: [ :e |
                AppToolWindow report: 'app ' , id printString , ': ' , e messageText.
                nil ].
        ^worker
    ]

    "── the window this app drives ──────────────────────────────────────

     ITS RENDER BLOCK ANSWERS A CACHE, and that is the whole trick. A render
     block must answer NOW, and the app VM answers WHEN IT ANSWERS; so the
     window renders whatever spec last arrived, and an arriving spec triggers
     the rerender that shows it. That is what a display server is: it draws
     the last frame it was given and never waits for the next one."
    register [
        AppToolWindow
            register: id title: title width: width height: height
            render: [ (AppVm named: id) lastSpec ]
            handler: [ :event :value | (AppVm named: id) sendEvent: event value: value ]
            paint: [ :tool :paneId :region |
                "Painting stays with the app: it owns the pixels, and A3's
                 surfaces are reachable from any VM by handle."
                (AppVm named: id) paintPane: paneId damage: region in: tool ].
        ^self
    ]

    "Ask the app VM for its first frame, then open. Opening BEFORE the spec
     arrives would realize an empty window and immediately rebuild it."
    open ^ <Boolean> [
        self isAlive ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        self requestSpec.
        Worker runLoopWhile: [ lastSpec isNil and: [ self isAlive ] ].
        lastSpec isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^(AppToolWindow named: id) open
    ]

    "── the two crossings ───────────────────────────────────────────────"

    requestSpec [
        ^self ask: (Array with: #spec)
    ]

    sendEvent: event <Symbol> value: aValue <Object> [
        ^self ask: (Array with: #event with: event with: aValue)
    ]

    "One crossing, and every one of them can fail. A dead worker is REPORTED
     and the window keeps its last frame — the alternative is a UI that dies
     with the app it was showing, which is the whole thing an app VM is for."
    ask: payload <Array> [
        self isAlive ifFalse: [
            failures := failures + 1.
            AppToolWindow report: 'app ' , id printString , ' is not running'.
            ^false ].
        [ worker send: payload onReply: [ :reply | self replyArrived: reply ] ]
            on: Error do: [ :e |
                failures := failures + 1.
                AppToolWindow report: 'app ' , id printString , ': ' , e messageText.
                ^false ].
        ^true
    ]

    replyArrived: reply <Object> [
        ((reply isKindOf: Array) and: [ (reply at: 1) == #spec ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self specArrived: (reply at: 2) ].
        ((reply isKindOf: Array) and: [ (reply at: 1) == #failed ])
            ifTrue: [
                failures := failures + 1.
                ^AppToolWindow report: 'app ' , id printString , ' handler: ' , (reply at: 2) ].
        ^self
    ]

    "A FRAME ARRIVED. It is a real AppNode — pickled across, not parsed — so
     everything downstream is the ordinary in-process path: normalize,
     validate, diff against what is on screen, patch what changed.

     TO MOVE THE DIFF INTO THE APP VM, which §3.2 wants eventually: have
     AppVmClient keep its own `lastSpec`, send `AppSpec diff:against:` ops
     instead of the tree, and call `apply:spec:` here instead of `rerender`.
     Both sides already have the whole model, so nothing else changes — the
     reason it is not done yet is that the ops path wants its own failure
     story for `the display and the app disagree about what is on screen`."
    specArrived: aSpec <Object> [
        aSpec isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        lastSpec := aSpec.
        (AppToolWindow named: id) isOpen ifTrue: [ (AppToolWindow named: id) rerender ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── painting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     A pane's pixels are the app's, and `AppSurfaces` is reached by HANDLE, so
     the app VM can be told which handle to draw into and do the drawing on its
     own thread. v1 keeps the paint call here and asks the app to do the work
     by RPC — the surface itself is shared, so nothing is copied."
    paintPane: paneId <Object> damage: region <Object> in: tool <Object> [
        self isAlive ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        ^self ask: (Array with: #event with: #paint
                          with: (Array with: paneId asString
                                       with: (tool paneHandle: paneId)
                                       with: region))
    ]

    "── the end of one ──────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    stop [
        (AppToolWindow named: id) isNil ifFalse: [ (AppToolWindow named: id) teardown ].
        worker isNil ifFalse: [ worker terminate ].
        worker := nil.
        ^self
    ]
]
